in 1964 tezuka osamu the father of manga  and author of astro boy wrote a chapter  called the greatest robot on earth  in this story a sultan built a robot  with the purpose of becoming the king of  all robots and by proxy ruler over the  modern world  but in order to achieve that dream this  evil horned creation  first had to triumph against the seven  most powerful robots on earth including  our hero astro boy  what ensued rapidly escalated from a  mere fight between robots into a grand  battle with the fate of the world at  stake as most stories of this type do  but just as much as tezuka osamu's work  can be labeled as shonenuken manga  stories made for young teens it can also  be argued that his repertoire holds all  of the ingredients needed for a much  darker and thematically remarkable story  as well  japanese textbooks even note the vast  array of deeper sub-genres his stories  explore  religion fine arts the universe  even going into osama's unabashed  discussions of the importance of life  futility of war  and the future of humankind  in the appropriate context even a story  so reminiscent of childhood nostalgia  and the ambition of a young boy could  certainly be twisted into a dark tale  and nearly 40 years later naoki  urasawa's melancholic brooding  reimagining of the greatest robot on  earth proved just that  in the murder mystery named after the  story's monstrous killer robot seeking  world domination  [Music]  what is uniquely interesting about pluto  is that though it might be chronicled in  the annals of time being known as a  masterfully warped reimagining of one of  tezuka osamu's greatest stories  i didn't know this context at all before  i read it  after i saw that pluto was only eight  volumes notably shorter and more tightly  plotted than any of naoki urasawa's  other more famous works like monster and  20th century boys  all i expected coming into this story  was another incredible suspenseful and  contemplative crowning literary piece to  add to urasawa's collection  and before i knew the background of this  story and after  that is still exactly what i got  though the original framework of this  manga should i say was built by the late  tezuka osamu urasawa fleshed out the  greatest man on earth in nearly every  way to create pluto to accommodate for  his darker style and unique prowess as a  mangaka  in his rendition this future take on our  world has developed robots just as  advanced if not more than humans  laws exist to protect robot rights even  blurring the line between them being  obedient servants to humans and being  their own equal autonomous inhabitants  of earth  but this advanced world is also in  recovery a great war in central asia was  incited in response to a rebellious  country's secret development of  immensely destructive robots  and ironically the rest of the world  sent their own robots believed to be  invincible unflinching and emotionless  the perfect tools for war  to massacre the entirety of this country  that got a bit too ahead of themselves  the seven most powerful robots in the  world were tasked with the job  mont blanc the beloved lumberjack and  heart of mother nature  north 2 a reclusive butler  heracles and brando the strongest  wrestlers on earth  epsilon a photon powered pacifist  gesekt a europol detective known for his  unrivaled deduction abilities  and of course adam or astro boy  after this war people of earth now sit  in a capricious state of both  gratefulness and reverence towards  robots like adam for being the forefront  of technology and embodiment of human  heroism  and also hatred towards robots as they  infringe closer and closer  onto the precipice of what makes us  humans unique  [Music]  in urasawa's pluto rather than pushing  adam as the narrative center of the  story as you would expect of a remake of  an astro boy comic rosawa actually tells  most of the story through gesekte the  detective an appropriate shift for this  mystery thriller take on pluto's battle  to become the greatest robot on earth  gesick the detective investigates the  string of unprecedented robot related  murders that pluto leaves in his  destructive wake beginning with the  beloved but still extremely powerful  robot mont blanc  all of the victims are adorned with  horns a symbol of the roman god of the  underworlds pluto and a suitable serial  killer-esque motif added in by urasawa  while gesic battles his own internal  monsters and comes to terms with what it  means to be a robot in society gesig  must also find and understand the horror  that is pluto one that he slowly  realizes is targeting him and the other  greatest robots on earth as well  in a scheme that is much greater than  anyone could have anticipated  pluto is a complex story i would argue  that it's urasawa's most complex that  i've read which could be due to the fact  that the majority of his worked on this  project went into thinking about how he  can expand on osama's original plot  without having to write that foundation  himself  pluto is very much its own iceberg at  the tip of the iceberg is the summation  of the knowledge of most people the fact  that pluto is a remake of an old astro  boy story arc small details like that  while a massive submerged part holding  societal subtexts and urasawa's own  philosophical flair  lies below the surface of it all  in fact i don't think that i alone could  fully encapture all of this in one video  i've provided a few great articles to  read about the series to understand it  further in the description which i read  through in an attempt to at least make a  dent on this titanic iceberg myself  but i will certainly attempt it and one  of the most pronounced and ever-present  themes permeating this story defining  the shifting balance between human and  robot and the most important when  attempting to present all of the other  ideas in pluto  is  [Music]  frustration great hatred hatred so  strong it could cause one to kill a man  these things nurture an ai  only a wrong mind can be perfect  this line from dr tenma the creator of  adam in the story takes a lot to unpack  at the end of the day we must remember  what this story is about  the greatest robot on earth  moving past jessica osamu's simple idea  of the greatest robot being the  strongest one of them all rosawa also  integrates our modern debates on what  makes technology great  what can be summed up as their ability  to imitate or transcend human function  pluto discusses this idea to a much  greater depth than most manga that touch  on this idea  for example in this story numerous laws  have been made to protect robot rights  crime against robots is just as illegal  as it is against humans and more so than  anything in pluto  robots have  emotion  typical robot stories delve into the gap  between humans and robots as this  ability to feel emotion but pluto does  the opposite and looks at what happens  when that gap separating them disappears  adam guessed all of the seven greatest  robots were created to imitate human  nature  humans hate they feel sadness so a  perfect robot should be able to do these  things as well  in a way this story reverts robots to  more of a different race than humans  rather than a lower species  there were subtle hints showing this and  there are also more obvious ones like  extremely on-the-nose comparisons of  anti-robot organizations to racial  supremacy groups  but i digress and i return to urasawa's  discussion of how a robot being a exact  imitation of a human what technically  makes them perfect is a contradiction in  and of itself  a robot kills a human at the beginning  of pluto's story and people begin  questioning its ai in response  throughout the rest of it  what went wrong  how is it possible that it killed a  human when robots are specifically coded  not to  wasn't it supposed to be perfect  and that is exactly the problem actually  as adam notes  a perfection that kills people  isn't that a human  that robot killing a person did not make  him any less perfect but actually better  than other robots because it is now even  closer to being human  when a robot begins to surpass its own  code even show emotion and its own  volition it becomes in a sense  perfect  it is quite ironically a flawless  imitation  of the very imperfect human  the humans in this story create robots  in their own image and strive to make  them indistinguishable from us but then  are surprised when they begin expressing  human traits of their own  this story is so dark because it makes  it clear that humankind's idea of right  and wrong perfect and imperfect is  severely skewed  the main cast of seven robots actually  feel like the morally best characters in  the entire series  until we slowly see them show more and  more humanity hatred and sorrow  and their pursuit of perfection i  suppose reversely becomes a horrible  sight to behold  urasawa masterfully shifts this balance  and makes us favor the robots rather  than humans  and nothing in this story embodies that  horror of humanity more than its  discussion of  [Music]  tezuka usamu first began his career as a  mangaka in 1947 in the years following  the second great world war and a period  of both immense strife and postwar  recovery for japan  it makes sense why this same background  being added to pluto would be an  appropriate homage to his work  robot stories typically glorify them as  machines of war tools for utter  destruction  but once again because pluto uniquely  gives its robots emotions or at least a  semblance of them and the ability to  fuel them it sheds an entirely different  light when they're sent to war  in central asia the mountain of corpses  that the seven sat upon after being  called the supposed victors were not  just robot bodies to them not just bits  and pieces of mechanical parts but their  own kind  it's even sadder than normal human war  because in this case the robots didn't  have any choice  adam guessex heracles mont blanc they  were pitted against the robots that a  rogue nation created they were forced to  commit genocide against their own kind  without say or volition in the matter  they were given enormous power  only for them to have to use it to  slaughter their own race  to punish a country from making weapons  of mass destruction the rest of the  world used those same very weapons  against them  is this  peace  there is nothing more futile in my  opinion than that repeating cycle of  hatred at the hands of humanity  osamu in the original story actually  includes a quote about this addressing  the seven most powerful robots asking  them  when will they sit in judgment of the  humans who created temporary life out of  a limitless lust for power and control  and then destroyed it  an important detail to note about our  seven main robots is that even though  they experience emotion  they don't understand it  heracles exists as a robot wrestler  after the war but finds that he can no  longer bring himself to destroy his  opponents as they normally do  questioning whether this is what humans  call pity  mont blanc questions what he's done as  he sits upon a battlefield of corpses  feeling what we might call regret  this is important to note because we as  readers feel such compassion towards  these poor beings who've been  manipulated into acting on the behalf of  humans  given the feeling of pain sorrow and  hatred that come with it  but aren't able to process it for what  it is  it's as if humans want to create robots  exactly like them and to create another  form of human but then continue to treat  them as nothing more than an  amalgamation of  parts it's how urasawa paints the robots  as the best characters in pluto and his  ability to expose humanity for his  horrors  that creates one of the deepest stories  i've ever read and one of the best ways  to honor the legacy of the father of  manga himself  despite being a rehashing of an old  story pluto still stands proud among  urasawa's other manga as an impressive  piece of literature it's the kind of  story where i think reading it multiple  times would greatly benefit anyone  seeking to fully understand its societal  implications and message for the world  it is the perfect example of what occurs  when two of the greatest minds come  together  and make something truly  beautiful  as always this has been the anime  culture corner i hope you enjoyed the  video don't forget to like and subscribe  for future in-depth show manga and  character analyses
 

